Total IP Control v2.0.0-beta37 - Longer lists, and a nicer settings menu
Copyright (c) 2026 Sysop Network

This is a BETA test build. It carries forward everything in beta36 and
every earlier 2.0.0 beta.

  This build replaces ALL THREE files: SNTIPCTL.DLL, SNTIPCTL.MSG and
  SNTIPCTL.MDF. The DLL and MSG must be installed together.


What is new in beta37:
  * 10 trusted proxies each for telnet and SSH (was 2).

  * 50 entries each in the IPv4 and IPv6 whitelists (was 10), shown 10
    to a page: N and P turn the page, and any entry's number (1-50)
    works from any page. "Connection Limit Whitelist (IPv4)" is now
    called "IPv4 Whitelist".

  * A smarter-looking menu: box-drawn title bars, the section list shows
    what each section holds ("3 of 50 in use", "2 settings, 1 proxy"),
    values are coloured (YES green, unset entries dim), a setting that
    cannot work yet shows why in red beside its value, and paged
    sections show "page 2 of 5" in their title bar.

  * Your existing proxies and whitelist entries carry over. The longer
    lists are kept in new records of SNTIPCTL.DAT; the first 2 proxies
    and first 10 IPv4 entries are also still kept where earlier builds
    look for them, so going back to an earlier build keeps those.


From beta36 (if you have not installed it):
  * The command is now /TIP (it was /TOTALIP).

  * The settings menu opens on six sections, like the Globals settings
    menu, in the order of the old configuration forms:

       1. General Settings                    8 settings
       2. Telnet Trusted Proxies              4 settings
       3. Connection Limit Whitelist (IPv4)  10 settings
       4. IPv6 Whitelist                     10 settings
       5. GeoIP Location                     16 settings
       6. SSH Settings                        6 settings

    Pick a section to see its settings with their current values,
    numbered from 1. Type a setting's number, and the menu shows its
    value and what is allowed; type the new value, or press Enter to keep
    it. A setting with fixed choices lists them by number. X goes back
    one step.

  * Sysops can also reach the same menu from a BBS menu item. In the
    Menu Tree editor, add an item that runs the Total IP Control module
    with the command line:

       MENU=SYSOPMENU

    Give the item a Sysop key so only you see it. MASTER access is
    needed, as for /TIP. MODULE=, MAXIP= and BYPASS= work as before for
    the per-module gateway; other MENU= names are reserved for later.

  * Help everywhere. ? at any prompt explains that setting; in a section,
    H and a number (H 2) explains a setting without opening it; H alone
    is a quick-start guide.

  * Changes apply and save at once (there is no Save step), and every
    change writes an Audit Trail line naming the Sysop, the section and
    the setting.

  * The menu tells you when a setting cannot do its job yet, for example
    "Block direct connections  YES (inactive: no proxy listed)".

  * All of the menu's text is in SNTIPCTL.MSG, so it can be reworded in
    the BBS's message editor like the rest.

  * The connection-limit and module-gateway refusal messages (CLIMIT,
    IPGDNY) use named variables (N!SNTUSERS, N!SNTMAXIP) in place of %s
    and %d, so their text can be reworded and reordered freely.

  * The DLL's file properties show the right version again (they had
    been stuck at beta31).


Where the old form settings are now:
  The telnet "trusted proxy enforcement" and "require trusted proxy"
  switches moved from General Settings into Telnet Trusted Proxies, next
  to the proxies they use. Profile fields are picked by name (ADDRESS1-
  ADDRESS4 or PHONE) instead of 1-5. Keys, text and addresses are cleared
  by entering -.


What is NOT changed:
  * No setting is removed or renamed, and every existing value carries
    over unchanged.
  * Connection handling (PROXY headers, blocking, limits, the SSH
    prx_ssh_ready() export from beta33/34) is unchanged, apart from how
    the refusal messages get their user list (now a named variable).


Installing:
  1. Stop the BBS.
  2. Copy SNTIPCTL.DLL, SNTIPCTL.MSG, SNTIPCTL.MDF and SNTIPCTL.RLN into
     your BBS installation directory (typically C:\BBSV10), replacing
     the old files.
  3. Restart. The Audit Trail at start-up should report v2.0.0-beta37.

  If you had reworded any Total IP Control message (CLIMIT, PRXBLK,
  IPGDNY, IPGGNT, MYIP, WHOHDR, WHOROW) in the message editor, the new
  SNTIPCTL.MSG replaces it: note your text before installing and redo it
  afterwards. In CLIMIT and IPGDNY, use N!SNTUSERS where %s was and
  N!SNTMAXIP where %d was.


How to test:
  1. Type /TIP as a Master user: the six sections should list with their
     setting counts. /TOTALIP no longer does anything.
  2. Add a Sysop menu item with command line MENU=SYSOPMENU; selecting it
     should show the same menu, and X should return you to that menu.
     MENU=ANYTHINGELSE should do nothing and leave a WARNING in the Audit
     Trail.
  3. Open each section; GeoIP Location (16 settings) should fit on one
     screen. In IPv4 Whitelist, N and P should page 1-10, 11-20 ... 41-50
     and wrap around; typing 37 from page 1 should open entry 37.
     After upgrading, your old proxies and whitelist entries should all
     be there.
  4. Change a yes/no setting and a number. Each should say "saved", show
     the new value, and leave an Audit Trail line naming the section.
  5. Change a list setting (General Settings 5): it lists the choices;
     pick one by number and another time by name.
  6. Add a telnet trusted proxy 10, an SSH trusted proxy 7, IPv4 entry
     50 and IPv6 entry 37, and clear (-) one. Restart the BBS and confirm
     they all persisted. The section list's counts should match.
  7. Type ? at a setting's prompt, H 2 in a section, and H in the list.
  8. No "More?" pause should appear anywhere in the menu.
  9. Trigger the connection limit (or a gateway limit) and check the
     refusal message still names the other users.
  10. Check that an existing gateway item (MODULE=... MAXIP=...) still
      works.


